**Item 09 is done and marked `- [x]`.** The loop stands at 10 of 14 solved.

**What was found.** The mining produced no conjecture worth having. Of 11,589 candidate formulas over 30 branching-aware invariants on 50,949 certified instances, 47 exceed the solver's bound on more than 5% of the 338 gap instances. Thirty-six of them are the theorem `optimum ≥ treewidth + 1` in disguise, two are fitted lower envelopes of that theorem and `lb_best` that never beat the pair, and the rest are fitted constants that look novel only on 60 to 125 customer graphs where treewidth is an unresolved interval. Seventy-seven percent of fitted candidates leak on grouped hold-out. The adversary, 297,000 exact-solver evaluations at 8 to 30 customers, broke nothing, and the section says plainly that this record is vacuous rather than clean.

**The one real object** is exact treewidth itself. It is a theorem the solver never computes, and it beats the certified lower bound on 199 of the 338 gap instances and 933 corpus instances, reaching tightness on 67. It is proposed as a `_lower_bound` component in §24 and not applied.

**A bug surfaced and was fixed.** The branch-lemma invariant from iteration 10 counted components that never touched the cut vertex as branches, and sat above the optimum on 347 rows. The theorem table's "must be 0" column caught it. The function is fixed with a regression test, and the 8,292 affected rows were recomputed in place.

**Kill verdict.** Literally not met, since a theorem survives. Met against the honest reference `max(lb_best, tw + 1)`: no candidate beats the proved bound on more than 0.1% of instances with exact treewidth. The bound work needs a new idea, not a new formula.

